Hybrid organic-inorganic lead halide perovskites are promising materials for cost-effective printable photovoltaics. As a result of the enhanced purity of the reagents1,2 and careful optimization of perovskite layer fabrication process,3–25 conversion efficiencies have increased significantly since the first report on perovskite solar cells in 2009.26 The highest certified PCE value for cell now exceeds $23\%$ .27,28 For large area devices, however, the certified PCE of a submodule still remains under $12\%$ .28 Further optimization of perovskite layer fabrication methods is therefore needed to obtain more efficient devices with larger active areas.29
Our previous contribution to the development of high efficiency perovskite solar cells include the introduction of purified $\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{X}_{2}$ $\mathbf{\nabla}\times=$ I, Br, Cl) precursor materials, now commercially available from
Tokyo Chemical Industry Co., Ltd. (TCI).1,2,30 We also established an effective one-step solution-based method for growing high quality methylammonium lead iodide perovskite layers using a mixed solvent approach. In this method, a precursor solution is made from methylammonium iodide and lead iodide dissolved in a 3:1 mixture of DMF and DMSO solvent. Thin films of the precursor solution are fabricated by spin coating. Towards the end of the spin program, toluene antisolvent is dripped onto the spinning films to promote the precipitation of a solid intermediate phase which is later transformed into perovskite by thermal annealing.2,23 Mixed DMF/DMSO solvent is used to balance the high solubility of the starting materials in DMF with the longer film drying times attained in low volatility solvents such as DMSO.21,31–
33 Drying time is an important process parameter since the antisolvent addition must be performed while the film is still wet (Figure S1). The precise timing of the antisolvent addition within this window is often critical in determining the film morphology.34 With 3:1 mixed solvent, for example, we found that optimal results required timing accuracy of within ± 1 s. Such a narrow process window for the antisolvent addition leads to variations in the film morphology due to timing errors and environmental factors, leading to poor reproducibility in the electrical performance of the fabricated devices (Figure S2).
Moving from a mixed DMF/DMSO solvent to pure DMSO would extend the drying window as a result of the reduced solvent volatility, but the reduced solubility of the starting materials normally makes fabrication of perovskite layers with the required thickness more difficult. To address this, we introduce a purified, solvent-intercalated precursor complex, ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ , which has enhanced solubility in DMSO compared to the conventional starting materials, CH3NH3I and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ . We demonstrate that the high purity and high solubility of ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF in DMSO leads to thick and dense perovskite layers ideal for solar cell fabrication, and show that the solvent-intercalated complex/DMSO precursor solution yields a significantly wider drying window compared with the previous mixed solvent method. Taking advantage of the wider available process window, an optimized fabrication method was developed which was found to yield devices with excellent power conversion efficiency. The electrical characteristics were found to be highly consistent as a result of the relaxed timing restrictions on the antisolvent addition. To demonstrate the potential of the optimized method for large area spin coated devices, we successfully fabricated a $22.0\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ , eight-cell module with $14.2\%$ PCE.
${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF was previously identified via a conventional solution process.35 The complex is of interest as a perovskite precursor material since the solubility of metal halides often increases when complexed with polar solvents.2,31,36 Our multisolvent recrystallization method for the fabrication of $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{D M F}$ , performed in a ${\sf N}_{2}$ filled glovebox, is as follows: $C H_{3}N H_{3}\vert$ and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ (1:1) were first dissolved in dry DMF at $70^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , and the resulting clear yellow solution was transferred to a glass tube. An antisolvent, chlorobenzene (Ph-Cl), was then layered over the DMF solution. Colorless crystals of ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF were harvested after one day (Figure 1a). ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF can be produced in industrial quantities with this method.37 We found in passing that ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}|$ is excluded from the complex when recrystallizing from $C H_{3}N H_{3}\vert$ and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ in DMSO solution. The product in that case was $\mathrm{[Pb}|_{2}(\mathsf{d m s o})_{2}]^{38}$ (Figure S3). Single crystal $\mathsf{x}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) analysis confirmed that the DMF molecule is intercalated between a planar $[\mathsf{P b}_{2}|_{6}]^{2-}$ layer and the $C H_{3}N H_{3}^{+}$ cation (Figure 1b). The ratio of ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ and DMF molecules is 1:1. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of a powder sample shows weight loss over a temperature range of $58-108^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ the sample changing from colorless to black in the interval (Figure 1c). The total measured weight loss, $10.2\%$ , is in good agreement with the calculated loss for the complete removal of a single DMF formula unit $(10.5\%)$ . The black crystals could be identified as solvent-free ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ perovskite by comparing the pristine and annealed samples with the simulated X-ray diffraction pattern of tetragonal $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}^{39}$ (Figure 1d).
The purity of ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF was checked with inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS), and, for comparison, $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ samples purchased from several suppliers were concurrently evaluated using glow discharge-mass spectrometry (GD-MS) (Figure S4). Based on the trace analysis of 70 elements, the purity of ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF was estimated to be $99.998\%$ $(>$ $99.99\%$ ), a high value similar to our purified $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ starting material (commercially available form TCI),30,37 and superior to $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ purchased from the other companies (Figures S5).
${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF dissolves more readily in DMSO $(\mathord{\sim}2.0~\mathsf{M}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ than the equivalent amounts of $C H_{3}N H_{3}l$ and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ starting materials $(\sim1.2\mathsf{M}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ) (Figure S6). The concentration of the precursor solution is kept slightly below saturation in order to extend film drying times, and improve reproducibility. At $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , the practical maximum working concentration for ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF was $1.4\mathsf{M}$ , about $30\%$ higher than a mixture of $C H_{3}N H_{3}\vert$ and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ in DMSO (1.1 M). Although the film mechanism and drying time are unchanged, the increased solubility permits thicker spin coated perovskite layers to be fabricated. The higher practical working concentration of the precursor solution is obtained as a direct result of the intercalated DMF released into the DMSO solvent as the complex dissolves. For 1.4 M CH3NH3PbI3·DMF in DMSO the DMSO:DMF ratio was estimated at 1:0.11.
Summarizing the above, high purity ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF precursor complex presents several advantages when used as a precursor material for solar cell device fabrication: First, the material has a well-defined and fixed stoichiometry of CH3NH3I and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ , so batch-to-batch composition will be consistent irrespective of weighing errors. Second, the amounts of impurities are extremely low, comparable to the best commercially available high-purity starting materials. Third, the material dissolves quickly and easily in DMSO. The process advantages of the long drying time, arising from the low volatility of DMSO, will be treated in detail in the following sections.
Perovskite layers were prepared in an inert gas filled glovebox $(H_{2}O$ and $0_{2}<0.1$ ppm) to minimize exposure to water and oxygen and ensure the optimal efficiency of the perovskite solar cells.1,2 DMF, DMSO, and toluene were dehydrated before use. ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF, prepared by the multi-solvent recrystallization method detailed above, was dissolved in DMSO to make a $1.4\mathsf{M}$ precursor solution. The spin program and antisolvent addition timing were both carefully tuned to obtain the best results with the complex precursor solution. The optimized method is as follows: First the precursor solution-covered substrate is spun at low rpm for 40 s to remove excess liquid and form a wet film. The rotation is then stopped and the film allowed to concentrate slightly under rest conditions for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . Finally, the film is spun at 5000 rpm for 20 s. Toluene antisolvent is dripped over the film beginning 2 s before the end of the program to induce precipitation of intermediate crystallites. The total process time of the optimized spin coating procedure is just over $90\ \mathsf{s}\ \qquad$ . After spin coating, the film was immediately annealed to form the solvent-free perovskite film. Here too it was found that a carefully optimized process yielded significant improvements in film quality. Our annealing method was performed on a hot plate in the glove box environment and followed a stepwise ramp with holding temperatures of $45~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 10 min, $55^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , $75^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 10 min, and $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ .2
In Figure 2, the advantages of the wider process window afforded by the longer drying time of DMSO are illustrated with reference to our previous one-step solution process using mixed DMF/DMSO solvents.2 Low rpm spin programs up to 70 s long could be inserted before the final 5000 rpm step, and antisolvent dripping could be performed at any point over a $\boldsymbol{{13\mathsf{s}}}$ interval (at $82\texttt{s-95}\texttt{s}$ ) with little change in film morphology or device performance. SEM images confirm the formation of dense and flat perovskite layers in each case (Figure 2b). The photovoltaic performance was also consistent (Figure S8, Table S1). This is in sharp contrast with the mixed DMF/DMSO solvent method, where only 1 s difference in the antisolvent dripping timing led to poor morphology. Varying the spin time of the initial 1000 rpm stage from 0 s to 40 s did not substantially effect film morphology (Figure S9). An optimized method is shown in Figure 2c, which was developed with view to facilitate large area devices and modules from blade coating and roll-to-roll processing techniques. The best results were obtained by low rpm spin step for an initial 40 s followed by a zero rpm rest period of $30{\mathsf{s}}$ , then a final $20{\mathsf{s}}$ stage at 5000 rpm with dripping of toluene 2 s before the end.
Perovskite solar cell devices were prepared with the following structure: Glass-FTO/compact- $\cdot\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ $(20\ n m)$ /mesoporous- $\cdot\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ (100 $\mathsf{n m})/\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}/2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}.$ -tetrakis(N,N-di- $\cdot p$ - methoxyphenylamine)-9,9ʹ-spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD) (200 nm)/Au $\left\langle80\mathsf{n m}\right\rangle$ . Details of the device fabrication are given in the supporting information. The perovskite layer for the devices was fabricated at room temperature by the optimized methods detailed in the previous section, using the same $1.4\mathsf{M}$ precursor solution of ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF in DMSO. The perovskite film thickness was $350~\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure 3a). The best performing cell showed $J_{\tt S C}=22.7$ $\mathsf{m A c m}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1.15\:\mathrm{V}$ , FF $=0.76$ , $\mathsf{P C E}=19.8\%$ (Figure 3c red line). Only very small hysteresis effects are observed. Incident photon-to-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra of the best performing and typical cells are shown in Figures S10 and S11, respectively. The integrated $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ obtained from IPCE measurement conforms closely to the $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ in $\jmath_{-}\chi$ curves. For comparison, reference cells were prepared at room temperature from a 1:1 mixture of $C H_{3}N H_{3}\vert$ and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ dissolved in DMSO to a working concentration of $1.1~\mathsf{M}$ and fabricated under the same process regime. The lower working concentration of ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}|$ and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ in pure DMSO reduces the thickness of the spin coated perovskite layers to $180~\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure 3b). For the best performing cell, $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}=21.8~\mathsf{m A c m}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1.15\:\mathrm{V}$ , $\mathsf{F F}=0.70$ , $\mathsf{P C E}=17.6\%$ was obtained (Figure 3c blue line). The lower short circuit current, and corresponding reduction in PCE, is attributed to higher optical transmittance of the perovskite layer. The working concentration was found to give optimal results. As a result of the shorter drying times it was not possible to get better performance with more highly concentrated DMSO precursor solutions. A major additional advantage of using ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF precursor solution is the consistency of the electrical performance. This is illustrated in Figure 3d, where the PCE distribution of 30 cells (forward scan) prepared from ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ ·DMF is compared with a similar batch of cells prepared from $C H_{3}N H_{3}\vert$ and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ . Confirmation of the tightly consistent performance is given by the individual histograms of $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ , $V_{\mathrm{{OC}}}$ , and FF (Figure S12). The unencapsulated devices showed good stability when stored in the dark in a glovebox under nitrogen atmosphere. Measurements were performed in air. As shown in Figure S13, $76\%$ of the maximum device efficiency $(>15\%$ PCE) is retained even after 500 days.
Summarizing the main findings: An optimized, highly reproducible spin coating method was developed for fabricating efficient perovskite solar cells (PCE $19.8\%$ and modules (PCE $14.2\%$ ) starting from a purified, solvent-intercalated precursor complex, $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{D M F}$ , dissolved in pure DMSO solvent. Taking advantage of the fast-acting solvation and high saturation concentration of the complex in DMSO, spin programs exceeding $90\ \mathsf{s}$ are possible, resulting in significantly relaxed timing for the critical antisolvent addition step. In the future, we hope to extend these promising results to flexible devices using plastic substrate and roll-to-roll fabrication.
